---
// Homepage "Selected work" (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section 4, item 2). Numbered
// "02" now, not "01" - per direct request ("change the order between
// selected works and design principles"), Principles now leads the
// homepage (see index.astro and Principles.astro's own comments).
// Currently Working On still sits directly below this section regardless
// of its numbered position.
//
// Real project titles are supplied (per direct message: "project 1 => Nine to
// Thrive, project 2 => Miles and Meals, project 3 => Kickstarter: Izumi"
// for Design, "a space journey through screentime, nexxus and plan A" for
// Experience) - each card's `fullTitle` (see ProjectCard.astro). Images
// still don't exist (Section 6), so the image slot stays a flat
// placeholder regardless.
//
// ProjectCard's title visibility has changed twice now: an early version
// only revealed the real name on hover, a follow-up asked for it to show
// always instead, and the most recent direct request ("the image to fill
// the entire card at first... reveals the part with the info while
// hovering") reverses that again - the title (and the rest of the info:
// tag, year, arrow) is back to hover-only, but the mechanism is new: a
// sliding overlay panel over a full-bleed image, not the old always-
// visible info block below a smaller image frame. See ProjectCard.astro's
// own header comment for the full reasoning, including why this also
// fixes the accordion's recurring collapsed-title clipping bug.
//
// Three groups now, not two - direct follow-up request: "change the
// order between group projects and individual work. change the title to
// experience design projects. individual work should be changed to
// digital design projects. add a 3rd category called motion design and
// add the izumi card to that." Izumi moves out of the design group into
// its own single-item "Motion design" group; the other two groups keep
// their existing projects, renamed and reordered - Experience design
// projects now leads (was second), Digital design projects second (was
// first, "Individual work"), Motion design new and last. Numbering is
// re-sequenced 01-06 in this new display order (not kept pinned to each
// project's old number) so the badges still read as one clean ascending
// sequence down the page rather than jumping around to preserve history
// nobody can see anyway.
//
// The Motion design group (Izumi) was removed again per direct request
// ("it is not relevant for my portfolio") - back to two groups, five
// projects. The open group's label/line/icon turn ink black, and the same
// on hover, so the active category reads clearly.
//
// DESIGN_VARIANCE: 9 (see CLAUDE.md "Experimental design dials") — this is
// the section's second rebuild in one session. The first (an asymmetric
// 7fr/5fr bento, one featured card per group) was a direct improvement on
// the original equal-width triplet, but per direct follow-up feedback the
// user wasn't fond of it and asked for "something that expands" instead -
// an Awwwards-style expanding accordion strip: narrow panels at rest,
// one panel growing to take most of the row on hover/focus while its
// siblings compress. Both groups (Design, Experience) use the same
// accordion device now, same as the bento before it used one asymmetric
// device for both rather than singling one group out.
//
// This replaces the bento entirely rather than layering on top of it -
// `ProjectCard.astro`'s own `featured` prop (which only existed to size
// one card per bento row) is gone; every card in an accordion row is
// visually equal until hovered, so there's no "lead item" concept left to
// carry. The accordion's collapsed/expanded look is applied here, from
// outside ProjectCard, via `:global()` overrides scoped under
// `.selected-work__accordion` - ProjectCard itself stays exactly the
// generic ticket-card module the brief specifies (image, number, tag,
// year, title, arrow), unaware that this one section reshapes it.
//
// Mobile/tablet fallback (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section 8 lists this as an open
// decision — flagging, not silently resolving): hover-driven expansion has
// no touch equivalent, so below min-width: 900px this drops to a plain
// equal-width stack of full, upright cards - the same "macro asymmetry
// drops to a plain stack below a width threshold" pattern already used
// for AboutDrives' scattered cards, Hero's own grid, and the bento this
// replaced.
//
// Follow-up pass, several direct requests at once:
// - The expanded panel's flex-grow dropped from 6 to 3 - "I don't like
//   how dramatically the project cards stretch, my images will not be
//   that big" - 6/8 of the row read as a much bigger reveal than a
//   modestly-sized real image would ever need to justify. A second
//   direct follow-up ("the stretching of the cards still feels too
//   plastic and extreme for my brand") took it down again, to 1.6 - see
//   the accordion's own rule below for the full reasoning on why 3 still
//   read as too much, not just a smaller version of the same number.
// - Each group label gained a thin rule beside it ("make... look more
//   delicate with a little fine line next to it").
// - Each group is now a real disclosure (a button toggling an
//   `aria-expanded`/height-animated panel), collapsed by default once
//   JS loads - "put the design individual work and experience design
//   below a pop-open so the user doesn't feel like they have to scroll
//   endlessly." Renders fully open in plain HTML/CSS (progressive
//   enhancement, same "if JS fails, everything still renders in its
//   final, reachable position" principle used throughout this project -
//   see Hero's own header comment) - the script collapses everything
//   *except the first group*, which stays open by default per direct
//   follow-up request, so the page doesn't open with zero work visible.
//
// Group labels shortened again per direct follow-up: "Design:
// individual work" / "Experience design: group projects" became plain
// "Individual work" / "Group projects," each with its own project count
// in parens - `design.length`/`experience.length`, not a hardcoded
// number, so the count can't silently drift out of sync with the array.
// - A custom cursor (the same arrow ProjectCard's own inline icon uses)
//   appears while hovering a project - "the arrow should appear as the
//   cursor upon hovering a project" - adapted from Gallery.astro's own
//   cursor, living here rather than inside ProjectCard.astro since it's
//   this section's own interaction, not a generic card behavior (same
//   reasoning as the accordion's own `:global()` overrides). The
//   spotlight that originally came with it (dimming the rest of the
//   page, matching Gallery's own version) is gone again per direct
//   follow-up ("I dont want the spotlight anymore for selected works") -
//   removed outright, not just disabled, along with the markup/script/
//   CSS it needed and had nothing else depending on it.
//
// A contact prompt sits directly under SectionMarker now - direct
// request ("in the selected work section add a cta where suited to
// contact me"), flagged before implementing per that same message's own
// instruction ("if you think this is a bad idea tell me before
// implementing and ask me with alternatives"): a full boxed CTA here
// would have made three separate contact/CTA moments on one homepage
// (Principles' own "Get to know me," this one, and the big ContactCTA
// band at the page's end), which risked reading as repetitive rather
// than confident. Given the choice, a small quiet text link ("Like what
// you see? Let's talk.") was picked over a full button or skipping it
// outright. Links to `#contact`, the same bare-hash convention Header's
// own nav Contact link already uses (resolves to whichever page's own
// ContactCTA is present).
//
// Sits after the last project group, as the section's closing line. Per
// direct follow-up ("the like what you see, and the currently working on
// should be closer together"), this line and CurrentlyWorkingOn's own
// asterisked line directly below the section are one footnote pair, so
// the section's bottom padding is trimmed to keep them close.
import SectionMarker from '../ui/SectionMarker.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../ui/ProjectCard.astro';

const experience = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Project 01',
    fullTitle: 'A Space Journey through Screentime',
    roleTag: 'experience design',
  },
  { number: '02', title: 'Project 02', fullTitle: 'Nexxus', roleTag: 'experience design' },
  { number: '03', title: 'Project 03', fullTitle: 'Plan A', roleTag: 'experience design' },
];

const digital = [
  { number: '04', title: 'Project 04', fullTitle: 'Nine to Thrive', roleTag: 'digital design' },
  { number: '05', title: 'Project 05', fullTitle: 'Miles and Meals', roleTag: 'digital design' },
];
---

<section class="selected-work" id="work">
  <div class="selected-work__inner">
    <SectionMarker
      index="02"
      label="Selected work"
      description="A mix of solo design work and collaborative experience-design projects."
    />

    <div class="selected-work__group">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="selected-work__toggle"
        aria-expanded="true"
        aria-controls="selected-work-experience"
      >
        <span class="selected-work__group-label">
          Experience design projects
          <span class="selected-work__group-count">({experience.length})</span>
          <span class="selected-work__group-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </span>
        <span class="selected-work__toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      <div class="selected-work__panel" id="selected-work-experience">
        <ul class="selected-work__accordion">
          {experience.map((project) => (
            <li>
              <ProjectCard {...project} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="selected-work__group">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="selected-work__toggle"
        aria-expanded="true"
        aria-controls="selected-work-digital"
      >
        <span class="selected-work__group-label">
          Digital design projects <span class="selected-work__group-count">({digital.length})</span>
          <span class="selected-work__group-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </span>
        <span class="selected-work__toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      <div class="selected-work__panel" id="selected-work-digital">
        <ul class="selected-work__accordion">
          {digital.map((project) => (
            <li>
              <ProjectCard {...project} />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="selected-work__cta">
      <span class="selected-work__cta-wink" aria-hidden="true">
        *
      </span>
      Like what you see?{' '}
      <a href="#contact" class="selected-work__cta-link">
        Let's talk.
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!--
  A sibling of <section>, not a descendant - same containing-block
  reasoning Gallery.astro's own cursor already documents (a `position:
  fixed` element needs to stay outside anything that might ever grow a
  transform, and .project-card itself gets one on hover). Reused here
  rather than duplicated verbatim: same two-element (wrapper + inner)
  split so GSAP's x/y positioning and the CSS scale/opacity never fight
  over `transform` on one element.
-->
<div class="selected-work__cursor" aria-hidden="true">
  <span class="selected-work__cursor-inner">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none">
      <path
        d="M4.5 11.5L11.5 4.5M11.5 4.5H5.5M11.5 4.5V10.5"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.3"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      />
    </svg>
  </span>
</div>

<script>
  import { gsap, prefersReducedMotion } from '../../utils/motion';

  // --- Pop-open disclosures ---
  // Renders fully open in plain HTML (aria-expanded="true", no inline
  // hidden state) so the projects are reachable if this script never
  // runs; collapses everything except the first group once it does - per
  // direct request, the page shouldn't open with zero projects visible.
  // GSAP's height:'auto' support (it measures the natural height, then
  // tweens toward it) is what makes an animated open/close possible
  // without a hand-rolled max-height guess.
  const reduceMotion = prefersReducedMotion();

  document
    .querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.selected-work__toggle')
    .forEach((toggle, index) => {
      const panel = toggle.nextElementSibling as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!panel) return;

      const startsOpen = index === 0;
      gsap.set(panel, { height: startsOpen ? 'auto' : 0, overflow: 'hidden' });
      toggle.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(startsOpen));
      toggle.classList.toggle('is-open', startsOpen);

      toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const isOpen = toggle.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
        toggle.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!isOpen));
        toggle.classList.toggle('is-open', !isOpen);

        if (reduceMotion) {
          gsap.set(panel, { height: isOpen ? 0 : 'auto' });
        } else {
          gsap.to(panel, {
            height: isOpen ? 0 : 'auto',
            duration: 0.5,
            ease: 'power2.inOut',
            onComplete: () => {
              // Once open, let the panel's own height stay auto rather than
              // a frozen pixel value from the tween - the accordion cards
              // inside can still change proportions on hover/focus, and a
              // fixed height would clip or leave a gap depending on which
              // way that goes.
              if (!isOpen) gsap.set(panel, { height: 'auto' });
            },
          });
        }
      });
    });

  // --- Custom "go" cursor, hovering a project card ---
  // Gated the same way as Gallery's own version: a fine pointer only
  // (this is a continuous mouse-follow effect with no touch equivalent),
  // and never under reduced motion. The spotlight that used to run
  // alongside this (dimming the rest of the page while a card was
  // hovered) is gone per direct request - just the cursor now.
  if (!reduceMotion && window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) {
    const cursor = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.selected-work__cursor');
    const cards = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.selected-work .project-card');

    if (cursor && cards.length) {
      // Hides the system pointer over a card, same as Gallery's own
      // `.gallery__viewport--custom-cursor .gallery__trigger` - the
      // custom circle is the only pointer visible there once this runs.
      document.querySelector('.selected-work')?.classList.add('selected-work--custom-cursor');

      const moveX = gsap.quickTo(cursor, 'x', { duration: 0.35, ease: 'power3.out' });
      const moveY = gsap.quickTo(cursor, 'y', { duration: 0.35, ease: 'power3.out' });

      cards.forEach((card) => {
        card.addEventListener('pointerenter', (event) => {
          gsap.set(cursor, { x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY });
          cursor.classList.add('is-active');
        });
        card.addEventListener('pointermove', (event) => {
          moveX(event.clientX);
          moveY(event.clientY);
        });
        card.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => {
          cursor.classList.remove('is-active');
        });
      });
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .selected-work {
    padding-block: var(--space-section) 1.5rem;
  }

  .selected-work__inner {
    max-width: var(--intro-max-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--intro-margin);
  }

  /* The other half of the footnote-and-reference pair (see
     .selected-work__cta-wink above) - a matching terracotta asterisk
     closing this section's own SectionMarker description, connecting it
     to the closing CTA's own leading asterisk. :global() and ::after
     rather than a change to SectionMarker.astro itself or its
     `description` prop (a plain string, deliberately not a slot - see
     that component's own header comment on why) - this is a one-off
     connection specific to this section, not a generic SectionMarker
     feature, so it's scoped here via `.selected-work` as an ancestor. */
  .selected-work :global(.section-marker__description)::after {
    content: ' *';
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    color: var(--color-accent-signal);
    transform: rotate(-8deg);
  }

  .selected-work__group {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  /* A quiet closing line, not a second boxed CTA - see the file header
     comment for why. Same fine-print register as the group labels
     (mono, text-secondary), so it reads as a soft aside rather than a
     competing headline. */
  /* Closes the section, and pairs with CurrentlyWorkingOn's asterisked
     line right below it, so the section's bottom padding is trimmed to
     keep the two close together. */
  .selected-work__cta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4em;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  /* Matches CurrentlyWorkingOn's own .currently__wink exactly - the
     homepage's second terracotta wink (see the file header comment),
     paired with the asterisk closing SectionMarker's own description
     below as a footnote-and-reference pair, per direct request ("give
     it an asterix like for currently working on, and add the asterisk
     too behind the selected works paragraph to connect them"). Leads
     the line rather than trailing it, matching CurrentlyWorkingOn's own
     asterisk placement after its own direct correction ("the asterix
     should be before currently working on and like what you see"). */
  .selected-work__cta-wink {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-accent-signal);
    transform: rotate(-8deg);
  }

  .selected-work__cta-link {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
    transition: color var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  .selected-work__cta-link:hover,
  .selected-work__cta-link:focus-visible {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .selected-work__cta-link:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* The whole label row is now a real button, toggling the panel below
     it open/closed - "put... below a pop-open so the user doesn't feel
     like they have to scroll endlessly." Full-width, plain-background so
     it doesn't read as a boxed control, just a clickable heading row. */
  .selected-work__toggle {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .selected-work__toggle:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* Same fine-print weight as the header/hero location tag
     (.site-header__status / .hero__status) - text-label (12px) and
     text-secondary, not text-nav (14px) and full ink - per direct
     request to match "the same fine font as the belgium tag". The fine
     line beside it (.selected-work__group-line) is a later addition -
     "make... look more delicate with a little fine line next to it" -
     a flex row so the line fills whatever width the label text itself
     doesn't need. */
  .selected-work__group-label {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .selected-work__group-line {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    height: var(--border-hairline);
    background: var(--color-text-secondary);
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  /* A touch quieter than the label text itself - the count is a small
     supporting fact, not part of the label's own reading rhythm. */
  .selected-work__group-count {
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* The open category, and any category being hovered, turns ink black
     so the active group reads clearly against the closed ones. */
  .selected-work__group-label,
  .selected-work__group-line,
  .selected-work__toggle-icon::before,
  .selected-work__toggle-icon::after {
    transition:
      color var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft),
      background-color var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft),
      transform var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  .selected-work__toggle[aria-expanded='true'] .selected-work__group-label,
  .selected-work__toggle:hover .selected-work__group-label {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .selected-work__toggle[aria-expanded='true'] .selected-work__group-line,
  .selected-work__toggle:hover .selected-work__group-line {
    background: var(--color-ink);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .selected-work__toggle[aria-expanded='true'] .selected-work__toggle-icon::before,
  .selected-work__toggle[aria-expanded='true'] .selected-work__toggle-icon::after,
  .selected-work__toggle:hover .selected-work__toggle-icon::before,
  .selected-work__toggle:hover .selected-work__toggle-icon::after {
    background: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* A plain plus/minus, drawn from two hairline strokes rather than an
     icon font or glyph - one rotates to vanish into the other on open,
     the classic "+" becomes "-" toggle read. */
  .selected-work__toggle-icon {
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 0.9rem;
    height: 0.9rem;
    margin-left: var(--space-24);
  }

  .selected-work__toggle-icon::before,
  .selected-work__toggle-icon::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: var(--border-hairline);
    background: var(--color-text-secondary);
    transition: transform var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  .selected-work__toggle-icon::after {
    transform: rotate(90deg);
  }

  .selected-work__toggle[aria-expanded='true'] .selected-work__toggle-icon::after {
    transform: rotate(0deg);
  }

  /* Mobile/tablet default: a plain equal-width stack of upright cards -
     see the file header comment on why hover-driven expansion doesn't
     get a touch fallback of its own. */
  .selected-work__accordion {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-32);
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    /* A fixed-height row is what makes an accordion read as one object
                                                                     instead of three cards of changing aspect-ratio - the height stays
                                                                     constant, only each panel's share of the width changes. 18rem, not
                                                                     the 30rem this first shipped with - per direct feedback the panels
                                                                     read as "quite tall" at that height, more like portrait cards than
                                                                     the short, horizontal, rectangular strip that's actually wanted. */
    .selected-work__accordion {
      display: flex;
      align-items: stretch;
      height: 18rem;
      gap: var(--space-16);
    }

    /* flex-grow (not width or flex-basis) is what's animated: every
                                                                     panel starts equal (grow: 1) and the hovered/focused one steps up
                                                                     to grow: 1.6 on hover, below. That number has come down twice now
                                                                     - 6, then 3, then this - per two rounds of direct feedback ("I
                                                                     don't like how dramatically the project cards stretch," then
                                                                     "still feels too plastic and extreme for my brand"). The second
                                                                     round wasn't just "3 is still too big, try a smaller number": a
                                                                     fixed-height row with a flex-grow-driven width change is
                                                                     literally stretching one rectangle's aspect ratio every tick of
                                                                     the transition (height pinned at 18rem, width changing), which is
                                                                     what reads as "plastic" regardless of how far it goes - a
                                                                     real photo cropped to one shape distorting toward a very
                                                                     different one doesn't look like a considered reveal, it looks
                                                                     like it's being pulled. 1.6 keeps that same distortion but makes
                                                                     it small enough (roughly 44% of the row instead of 60%, versus
                                                                     each panel's 33% at rest) that it reads as a gentle emphasis
                                                                     rather than an elastic snap - and the transition itself slowed
                                                                     from 0.6s to 0.8s so the motion itself reads calmer, not just
                                                                     smaller. --motion-easing-soft (not a hand-typed cubic-bezier
                                                                     literal - see that token's own comment (global.css) for why this
                                                                     exact curve is shared across several places instead of
                                                                     copy-pasted) already eases gently; the extra time gives it room
                                                                     to actually read as gradual instead of quick-then-eased. */
    /* Fixed basis assuming a 3-panel row (the largest group, Experience
                           design), not flex: 1 1 0% - per direct request that a group with
                           fewer than 3 projects ("Digital design projects" has 2, "Motion
                           design" has 1) still uses the same per-panel width as a 3-panel
                           row instead of stretching to fill the whole row. flex-grow: 0 at
                           rest means a shorter row (2 or 1 panels) just leaves the
                           remaining width empty rather than inflating each panel to fill
                           it - a lone Motion design card now reads as one panel-width, not
                           a full-width banner. flex-shrink: 1 still lets siblings compress
                           when the hovered panel grows (below). */
    .selected-work__accordion > li {
      flex: 0 1 calc((100% - 2 * var(--space-16)) / 3);
      min-width: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      transition: flex-grow 0.8s var(--motion-easing-soft);
    }

    /* Temporarily disabled per direct request ("remove the stretching
                       effect temporarily from all the cards") - every panel now just
                       holds its own fixed basis above, hover or not. Left commented
                       rather than deleted so the accordion-expand behavior (and all the
                       reasoning above about why 1.6/0.8s were chosen) is one uncomment
                       away from coming back, not lost.
                    .selected-work__accordion > li:hover,
                    .selected-work__accordion > li:focus-within {
                      flex-grow: 1.6;
                    }
                    */

    /* The card fills its panel's full box instead of holding its own
                             aspect-ratio - same "stretch to fill a flex/grid cell" technique
                             the bento's featured card used before this replaced it.
                             ProjectCard.astro was rebuilt (direct request: "the image to fill
                             the entire card at first... reveals the part with the info while
                             hovering") around a full-bleed image with its own hover-reveal
                             info overlay - it no longer has a separate frame/body split or a
                             collapsed "spine label" state to maintain here at all. That
                             rebuild is what actually fixes the clipping bug this accordion
                             kept reintroducing across three earlier attempts (see AGENTS.md):
                             the writing-mode column-budget mechanism that caused it doesn't
                             exist anymore, there's nothing left to widen a fourth time. The
                             card's own `:hover` fires the moment the pointer is over it, which
